# The Weaver of Night

Long ago, when the world was young, the sky was empty and black. The Sun ruled the day with blazing pride, but when she descended to rest, darkness swallowed everything. People trembled in the endless night, unable to see their hands before their faces.

A young weaver named Stella wept for her people's fear. She climbed the highest mountain, carrying her loom and her finest silver thread. "Sun," she called out, "you shine so brilliantly, yet you share none of your light with the night!"

The Sun, moved by the weaver's courage, plucked a thousand fragments from her own radiance and placed them in Stella's basket. "These are precious," the Sun warned. "Use them wisely."

Stella worked through many cycles of day and night, weaving the fragments of sunlight into her silver thread. She cast her thread across the dark sky, creating intricate patterns. Where each knot bound the thread, a fragment of sunlight caught fast, twinkling eternally.

When Stella finished, the night sky blazed with countless stars. The people rejoiced, no longer afraid of darkness. The gods, witnessing such selflessness, transformed Stella herself into a constellation, where she continues her eternal vigil, ensuring the stars never fade.
