In ancient times, a mighty river flowed from the mountains to the sea, proud of its endless giving. It watered crops, quenched thirst, and carried boats. "I am the greatest giver," it declared, "for I ask nothing in return."

But the river grew weaker each year, its waters diminishing. The Spirit of the Mountain watched with concern and finally spoke: "Noble river, you give endlessly but refuse all gifts. The rain clouds wish to replenish you, but you push them away. The tributaries offer their waters, but you reject them as charity."

